+/* Hades: what the pictures of dvbridge_render_hdr10() and dvbridge_render_plain() are copied to: the
+ * levels of that framebuffer (dvbridge_gl_copy_levels()) and the range the copy writes. Where that is
+ * a 10-bit fixed-point target in full range (levels 1023, not limited), a picture is kept as the
+ * codes the copy writes, in an RGB10_A2 texture of the GPU: the output hook quantizes it as the copy
+ * does (COPY_FRAGMENT), and the copy of a code is then that code, the same bytes as from the float
+ * texture. A quarter of the memory, and of what each copy reads. Any other target (floating point, 8
+ * or 12 bits, the limited range, where the copy computes from the value), a GPU without that format,
+ * and a renderer that is not told: the float texture, as always. From the next picture drawn; the
+ * one kept in the other form is forgotten (dvbridge_render_hdr10_last() is NULL until one is drawn).
+ * True when the pictures are kept as codes. */
+#define DVBRIDGE_RENDER_HDR10_STORE 1
+bool dvbridge_render_hdr10_store(struct dvbridge_renderer *renderer, int levels, bool limited);
 /* Hades: a renderer for dvbridge_render_hdr10() alone, without the 3840x2160 picture that the
  * transport is packed from (132 MB that such a renderer never uses). It keeps two pictures of the
  * size of the output instead, the one shown and the one being drawn (132 MB each at 3840x2160). */
